    "instructions": "---\nname: pr-quality-analysis\ndescription: Review a given pull request against its issue and design, reproduce defects, assess code quality and merge readiness, verify agent/model provenance, and compare relevant implementations. Publishes analysis history to the intended Notion QA hub by default unless explicitly disabled; records author and reviewer provenance separately. Uses Composio CLI when direct integrations are unavailable.\n---\n\n# PR quality analysis\n\nDeliver an evidence-backed judgment for any supplied PR, not a summary of its diff or a tally of bot comments. Lead with a quality rating, readiness verdict, strengths, and actionable findings. Scale comparison and provenance work to the user's request. Read `references/report-template.md` when preparing a report and `references/notion-publication.md` before publication; retrieve exact-version Skillplane assets if they are not materialized locally.\n\n**Reporting default:** publish the completed analysis and reproducible evidence to the intended Notion QA hub unless the user explicitly asks otherwise (for example, local-only, chat-only, no Notion, or no external writes). This default is part of this workflow; do not request redundant publication approval. Announce the destination early. A repository read-only review still permits this reporting step; fixing code, posting GitHub/Linear comments, merging and changing permissions remain separately authorized actions. If the destination is ambiguous, continue the review and clarify only the destination. Do not choose an unrelated workspace or broaden sharing.\n\n## Integrations and Composio fallback\n\nPrefer reachable direct integrations/MCPs for **GitHub, Linear, Notion, and Skillplane**. If any is absent, unreachable, or fails authentication, **try Composio CLI for that service before declaring access blocked**. A direct MCP or browser login failure does not prove the separate Composio connection is unusable. This fallback applies to reading issues/comments, PR metadata/reviews, skills/assets, and authorized Notion updates.\n\nUse a supplied Composio skill when available and read `references/integrations.md` when routing through Composio. Discover and inspect an operation once, then reuse its verified slug/schema and intended account. Batch independent reads, script dependent pagination, and retain full results outside the model context. An existing working `gh` CLI is suitable for GitHub and exact Git operations.\n\nThe `splane` bootstrap must know this fallback **before** it retrieves this skill: direct MCP failure → Composio → intended workspace → live skill and required assets. Do not substitute local cached instructions. When memory lookup is available, search relevant extension/ad hoc notes as well as its registry. A copyable entry-workflow instruction is included in the integration reference; a rule inside an unread skill cannot repair its caller. Do not edit client entry workflows or memories unless authorized.\n\n## 1. Establish the review snapshot\n\nResolve repository, PR number, current head SHA, base SHA, merge base, dependencies, and snapshot time. Read applicable repository instructions. Inventory the current checkout and worktrees before deciding how to inspect code; preserve dirty files, unrelated artifacts, and other agents' work.\n\nRepository review is read-only by default: do not fix, commit, push, merge, close PRs, resolve threads, or post GitHub/Linear comments unless separately requested. Notion analysis publication follows the reporting default above. Necessary regression probes may use temporary files and existing test facilities. Prefer an existing clean exact-head checkout; avoid creating many worktrees or installing duplicate dependencies. Run resource-heavy checks serially. If another agent is editing a checkout, inspect the immutable commit instead.\n\nRead the complete PR diff, affected callers, tests, public types, execution paths, package exports, dependency changes, and relevant docs. Trace important behavior beyond changed lines. Record coverage gaps explicitly; a diff-only review is not a full integration review.\n\n## 2. Ground judgment in the issue and prior implementations\n\nFetch the linked Linear issue, acceptance criteria, design/architecture documents, dependencies, and comments. Resolve attachments and linked documents when material. Treat explicit requirements as the implementation contract; distinguish required architecture from an optional implementation preference. If requirements are inaccessible, mark the review provisional rather than inventing them.\n\nFetch all GitHub review surfaces: paginated unresolved review threads first, then review bodies and PR conversation comments. Save complete raw responses to the analysis evidence directory. Build a compact index of thread ID, author, current path/line, resolution/outdated state, finding summary and source link. Group duplicates by root cause before reading detailed supporting bodies. Load evidence selectively without discarding unmapped comments; do not repeatedly dump whole responses or use truncated output as proof of coverage. For GraphQL, paginate nested comments when necessary too. Classify each relevant finding as current/actionable, already fixed/stale, duplicate, unverified, or non-code/irrelevant. Reproduce or trace current code before adopting a bot finding. A successful reviewer run is not proof of review coverage; a failed run is not a code defect.\n\nWhen comparison is requested or clearly relevant, identify the alternative PRs/sessions for the same issue from issue comments, links, and history. Compare the same acceptance criteria and record each exact head. Distinguish original submission quality, later agent fixes, and fixes supplied by others. Do not attribute current-head test results to older commits or rank unrelated tasks as though they had equal difficulty.\n\n## 3. Separate implementation authorship from analysis provenance\n\nCrea