its own success labels. For example, a negative security fixture must demonstrate that the handler did not execute, not just that a returned lifecycle field says it was rejected.\n\nExercise the public failure path when relevant: malformed or failed input → coordinator → artifact writer/renderer → publisher/consumer, not merely a helper returning an error label. For report/validation products, probe missing or invented evidence, invalid enums, forged verdicts, stale receipts and source identity independently. Mock external mutations and use dummy credential sentinels; review authorization does not authorize live publication to GitHub. Test documented consumer commands/protocols rather than trusting README claims.\n\nProbe relevant boundaries: failure and cancellation paths, partial cleanup and retries, authorization before execution, malformed inputs, schema/reference recursion, untrusted keys as well as values, serialization consistency, generated-project dependencies, and public-runtime compatibility. Choose cases based on the PR's actual contract; this is not a mandatory checklist for unrelated domains.\n\nFor each finding, capture severity (P0 critical, P1 high, P2 material defect, P3 minor), trigger, actual behavior, expected contract, user impact, current file/line or commit link, evidence status, and the narrow complete fix. Deduplicate root causes. Separate reproduced defects from code-traced risks and hypotheses. Failed probes caused by wrong aliases, stale built packages, native ABI mismatches, or missing fixtures are not proof of a product defect.\n\nRun proportionate type/build/package or release gates. Record commands, runtime, exact head, counts, and failures. Retain minimal probe source, fixtures/setup, stdout/stderr, exit status and dependency provenance alongside results; identify reused dependencies versus a clean lockfile install. Distinguish a fixture repository SHA from the PR source SHA. Redact secrets before preserving or uploading evidence. Distinguish local tests, CI on that head, unrelated baseline failures, environmental blocks, and unrun checks. A green focused suite does not establish release readiness. If the head changes during review, identify which results became stale and refresh only what is needed; do not enter an open-ended review loop.\n\n## 5. Rate quality separately from readiness\n\nUse these default dimensions, adapting transparently if the task requires it:\n\n| Dimension | Weight | Evidence |\n|---|---:|---|\n| Issue/design fidelity | 30% | Acceptance coverage and documented architectural constraints |\n| Correctness and safety | 30% | Runtime behavior, failure paths, data/security boundaries |\n| Architecture and maintainability | 20% | Shared production paths, clear ownership, appropriate scope |\n| Verification quality | 15% | Independent regression coverage and meaningful gates |\n| Delivery completeness | 5% | Public contracts, docs, packaging and migration/dependency needs |\n\nScore dimensions 0–10 and show a short rationale; round the overall rating to the nearest 0.5. Anchor ratings: 9–10 exceptional and well verified, 7–8.5 strong foundation with bounded gaps, 5–6.5 material gaps, below 5 major contract/correctness problems. If evidence is insufficient, mark the rating provisional or unscored instead of treating missing access as poor code.\n\nAn average never overrides a blocker. Report **ready**, **needs fixes**, **needs review/verification**, **waiting on dependency**, or **blocked evidence**, with reasons. A 7.5/10 PR can still be unsafe to merge. Credit strong original work even when follow-up fixes are needed.\n\nFor harness/model comparisons, retain issue-level paired evidence, sample counts, uncertainty, original-versus-remediated scores, and model/harness confounding. Keep aggregate scorecards separate from issue-analysis pages. Do not present a few mixed tasks as a controlled benchmark.\n\n## 6. Deliver and publish analysis history\n\nUse the report template for a