anges, and relevant docs. Trace important behavior beyond changed lines. Record coverage gaps explicitly; a diff-only review is not a full integration review.\n\n## 2. Ground judgment in the issue and prior implementations\n\nFetch the linked Linear issue, acceptance criteria, design/architecture documents, dependencies, and comments. Resolve attachments and linked documents when material. Treat explicit requirements as the implementation contract; distinguish required architecture from an optional implementation preference. If requirements are inaccessible, mark the review provisional rather than inventing them.\n\nFetch all GitHub review surfaces: paginated unresolved review threads first, then review bodies and PR conversation comments. Save complete raw responses to the analysis evidence directory. Build a compact index of thread ID, author, current path/line, resolution/outdated state, finding summary and source link. Group duplicates by root cause before reading detailed supporting bodies. Load evidence selectively without discarding unmapped comments; do not repeatedly dump whole responses or use truncated output as proof of coverage. For GraphQL, paginate nested comments when necessary too. Classify each relevant finding as current/actionable, already fixed/stale, duplicate, unverified, or non-code/irrelevant. Reproduce or trace current code before adopting a bot finding. A successful reviewer run is not proof of review coverage; a failed run is not a code defect.\n\nWhen comparison is requested or clearly relevant, identify the alternative PRs/sessions for the same issue from issue comments, links, and history. Compare the same acceptance criteria and record each exact head. Distinguish original submission quality, later agent fixes, and fixes supplied by others. Do not attribute current-head test results to older commits or rank unrelated tasks as though they had equal difficulty.\n\n## 3. Separate implementation authorship from analysis provenance\n\nCreate an analysis ID for each fresh assessment, including repeat analyses of the same PR and same head. Preserve it for publication retries. Capture analysis start/completion time, repository/PR, reviewed head/base/merge-base, source snapshot identity, scope, skill slug/version, reviewer client/harness and version, reviewer provider/model/version, reasoning setting, execution environment, task/session ID or link, and attribution source/confidence. Use explicit unknown values where unavailable; never infer hidden metadata. Distinguish the reviewer model from the implementation author model. Record mixed reviewer turns or delegated contributions when material. See the report template for the metadata shape.\n\nA GitHub author or branch name does not establish the implementation model. Correlate issue comments, PR links, session identifiers, transcript metadata, and commits. For local Codex work, use available task/session tools or narrowly search the known session ID under the local Codex session directory. Do not broadly dump unrelated conversations.\n\nRecord harness/client, execution environment, exact model/version when available, source, and confidence. Label user-reported attribution separately from metadata-confirmed attribution. A session may switch models: attribute individual turns or commit ranges, or mark it mixed. Never infer a model from code style or from the model currently performing this review.\n\nUse transcripts to explain observable decisions: requirement missed, shortcut chosen, test omitted, mistaken assumption, and whether later evidence changed the approach. Quote/link the decisive evidence. If the transcript is unavailable, say so; code alone cannot prove why an agent chose an approach. Do not turn one weak PR into a universal claim about a model or harness.\n\n## 4. Verify behavior independently\n\nRun focused existing tests first and add small independent probes for suspected defects. Avoid tests that merely mirror the implementation or trust its own success labels. For example, a negative security fixture must demonstrate that the handler did not execute, not just that a returned lifecycle field says it was rejected.\n\nExercise the public failure path when relevant: malformed or failed input → coordinator → artifact writer/renderer → publisher/consumer, not merely a helper returning an error label. For report/validation products, probe missing or invented evidence, invalid enums, forged verdicts, stale receipts and source identity independently. Mock external mutations and use dummy credential sentinels; review authorization does not authorize live publication to GitHub. Test documented consumer commands/protocols rather than trusting README claims.\n\nProbe relevant boundaries: failure and cancellation paths, partial cleanup and retries, authorization before execution, malformed inputs, schema/reference recursion, untrusted keys as well as values, serialization consistency, generated-project dependencies, and public-runtime compatibility. Choose cases based on the PR's actual contract; this is not a mandatory checklist for unrelated domains.\n\nFor each finding, capture severity (P0 critical, P1 high, P2 material defect, P3 minor), trigger, actual behavior, expected contract, user impact, current file/line or commit link, evidence status, and the narrow complete fix. Deduplicate root causes. Separate reproduced defects from code-traced risks and hypotheses. Failed probes caused by wrong aliases, stale built packages, native ABI mismatches, or missing fixtures are not proof of a product defect.\n\nRun proportionate type/build/package or release gates. Record commands, runtime, exact head, counts, and failures. Retain minimal probe source, fixtures/setup, stdout/stderr, exit status and dependency provenance alongside results; identify reused dependencies versus a clean lockfile install. Distinguish a fixture repository SHA from the PR source SHA. Redact secrets before preserving or uploading evidence. Distinguish local tests, CI on that head, unrelated baseline failures, environmental blocks, and unrun checks. A green focused suite does not establish release readiness. If the head changes during review, identify which results became stale and refresh only what is needed; do not enter an open-ended review loop.\n\n## 5. Rate quality separately from readiness\n\nUse these default dimensions, adapting transparently if the task requires it:\n\n| Dimension | Weight | Evidence |\n|---|---:|---|\n| Issue/design fidelity | 30% | Acceptance coverage and documented architectural constraints |\n| Correctness and safety | 30% | Runtime behavior, failure paths, data/security boundaries |\n| Architecture and maintainability | 20% | Shared production paths, clear ownership, appropriate scope |\n| Verification quality | 15% | Independent regression coverage and meaningful gates |\n| Delivery completeness | 5% | Public contracts, docs, packaging and migration/dependency needs |\n\nScore dimensions 0–10 and show a short rationale; round the overall rating to the nearest 0.5. Anchor ratings: 9–10 exceptional and well verified, 7–8.5 strong foundation with bounded gaps, 5–6.5 material gaps, below 5 major contract/correctness problems. If evidence is insufficient, mark the rating provisional or unscored instead of treating missing access as poor code.\n\nAn average never overrides a blocker. Report **ready**, **needs fixes**, **needs review/verification**, **waiting on dependency**, or **blocked evidence**, with reasons. A 7.5/10 PR can still be unsafe to merge. Credit strong original work even when follow-up fixes are needed.\n\nFor harness/model comparisons, retain issue-level paired evidence, sample counts, uncertainty, original-versus-remediated scores, and model/harness confounding. Keep aggregate scorecards separate from issue-analysis pages. Do not present a few mixed tasks as a controlled benchmark.\n\n## 6. Deliver and publish analysis history\n\nUse the report template for a compact chat verdict plus detailed evidence. State what was checked, strengths, remaining defects, dependency order where relevant, and limits. Preserve a dated analysis snapshot even when the same PR/head was analyzed before; publication retry is not a new analysis.\n\nPublish to Notion by default unless explicitly disabled. Follow `references/notion-publication.md`: reuse the intended hub, issue and PR record; add a distinct analysis child record or section identified by analysis ID; preserve prior findings, scores and reviewer metadata; link reproducible evidence with portable references. Keep the issue comparison at the top and implementation detail/history below. An implementation record may point to the latest completed analysis, but never silently overwrite history or average conflicting analyses. Label same-head reviewer disagreements and new-head re-reviews distinctly.\n\nKeep aggregate harness/model scorecards separate. Distinguish author-performance records from reviewer-analysis records, and do not count multiple analyses of one implementation as independent author samples. Do not update aggregates without a stated denominator and supported attribution.\n\nVerify publication by reading back content **and properties**: analysis ID, full finding set, score/verdict, reviewer/author distinction, reviewed SHA, source/evidence links, relations and history. Report the Notion links only after verification. If both direct access and Composio are blocked, deliver the full local review/evidence, state the exact publication blocker and any partial writes, and retain the same analysis ID for retry. Do not claim that Notion is unchanged if a partial write succeeded.\n\nStop after the requested analysis and its reporting. Fixing or merging is separate unless included in the user's instructions.\n",
