{"name":"ski4-architecture","result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"id\":\"bab21574-5c72-45b3-83a7-cd46281d95e6\",\"title\":\"Architecture & Delivery Plan — SKI-4: Clarify MCP authorization permissions and workspace identity\",\"content\":\"# Architecture & Delivery Plan\\n\\nBacklog issue: [SKI-4 — Clarify MCP authorization permissions and workspace identity](<https://linear.app/21n/issue/SKI-4/clarify-mcp-authorization-permissions-and-workspace-identity>)\\n\\n## Current-state constraints\\n\\n* Authentication is performed through AuthFn's OAuth authorization flow.\\n* The MCP resource is `https://mcp.skillplane.dev/mcp`.\\n* OAuth scopes are ultimately enforced server-side; consent copy must reflect those checks.\\n* A Skillplane user can belong to multiple personal or organization workspaces.\\n* Existing OAuth clients and PKCE S256 behavior must remain compatible.\\n* The current grant model should be treated as user-centric until code and schema inspection prove otherwise.\\n\\n## Proposed architecture\\n\\n### 1. Canonical scope-presentation catalog\\n\\nCreate one typed catalog keyed by scope identifier. Each entry should include:\\n\\n* short consent description;\\n* optional expanded explanation;\\n* permission group;\\n* sensitivity or warning metadata where useful;\\n* documentation reference;\\n* ownership and review expectations.\\n\\nThe consent page consumes this catalog. Tests compare the catalog with the registered/allowed scopes so a newly enforceable scope cannot appear with generic fallback copy unnoticed.\\n\\n### 2. Server-derived consent view model\\n\\nThe authorization server should construct a view model containing only trusted values:\\n\\n* requesting client identity and display metadata;\\n* requested and validated scopes;\\n* resource audience;\\n* validated callback summary;\\n* authenticated user summary;\\n* effective workspace-reach mode;\\n* eligible workspace choices, only when selection is supported;\\n* authorization transaction identifier protected from tampering.\\n\\nThe browser must not be the authority for client, user, scopes, resource, or workspace membership.\\n\\n### 3. Identity display\\n\\nResolve the authorizing user from the active authenticated server session and expose only presentation-safe fields. “Switch account” should invalidate or abandon the pending consent state and restart authentication so approval cannot accidentally attach to the previous identity.\\n\\n### 4. Workspace-reach resolver\\n\\nAdd an explicit resolver that reports the actual reach of the pending grant:\\n\\n* a bound workspace;\\n* all currently accessible workspaces;\\n* or membership-evaluated access at request time.\\n\\nThe resolver's output drives both user-facing copy and audit metadata. Unknown or inconsistent reach should fail closed rather than render a reassuring but inaccurate message.\\n\\n### 5. Optional workspace-bound grant\\n\\nIf product and architecture review confirm workspace selection, implement it end to end:\\n\\n1. The server lists only workspaces the authenticated user may authorize.\\n2. The server validates the selected workspace and current membership.\\n3. The selection is bound to the pending authorization transaction.\\n4. The grant or access token carries a tamper-resistant workspace restriction.\\n5. The MCP server enforces that restriction for `workspaces_list` and every workspace-aware tool.\\n6. Audit records include the authorized workspace identifier and reach mode.\\n7. Changing workspace requires a new or explicitly amended authorization flow.\\n\\nDo not ship a selector if any MCP operation can bypass the restriction.\\n\\n## Component responsibilities\\n\\n### AuthFn / OAuth service\\n\\n* Authenticate the user and validate client, callback, scopes, resource, and PKCE.\\n* Build the consent view model.\\n* Bind any selected workspace to the authorization transaction and resulting grant.\\n* Emit authorization audit events without credentials or secrets.\\n\\n### Skillplane domain service\\n\\n* Resolve presentation-safe user identity and accessible workspaces.\\n* Validate current membership and authorization eligibility.\\n* Supply canonical workspace labels and personal/organization distinctions.\\n\\n### Consent UI\\n\\n* Render trusted client, identity, scope, resource, callback, and workspace data.\\n* Provide accessible approve, deny, switch-account, and optional workspace-selection controls.\\n* Never infer reach or authorization semantics from browser state.\\n\\n### MCP server\\n\\n* Validate the access token/resource audience.\\n* Enforce scopes and any workspace restriction on every tool.\\n* Filter workspace enumeration consistently.\\n* Fail closed on missing, invalid, or inconsistent restriction data.\\n\\n## Data-model decision\\n\\nWorkspace restriction storage is intentionally not prescribed until the existing grant and token model is inspected. Candidate designs:\\n\\n* signed workspace claim in the access token;\\n* durable workspace association on the OAuth grant with token introspection;\\n* opaque authorization details referenced by the grant.\\n\\nSelection criteria include revocation behavior, token size, membership changes, audit needs, latency, and compatibility with existing clients. The chosen model must make the restriction authoritative, centrally testable, and non-bypassable.\\n\\n## Security and privacy requirements\\n\\n* Preserve CSRF, replay, state, PKCE, redirect URI, and resource-indicator protections.\\n* Revalidate workspace membership at authorization and define behavior when membership later changes.\\n* Prevent open redirects in account-switch and cancellation paths.\\n* Do not expose full membership lists or unnecessary identity attributes in logs or page source.\\n* Never log authorization codes, access tokens, refresh tokens, or PKCE verifiers.\\n* Fail closed when the requested workspace, user session, client, or transaction is stale.\\n* Ensure permission descriptions do not understate destructive or publishing capabilities.\\n\\n## Reliability and compatibility\\n\\n* Existing clients that do not request workspace binding must continue to authorize under a documented reach mode.\\n* Generic scope fallback copy should be treated as an observable error for registered production scopes.\\n* Consent rendering should remain usable if optional client branding fails.\\n* Authorization restarts must not reuse stale user/workspace selections.\\n\\n## Delivery phases\\n\\n### Phase 1 — Truthful copy, identity, and reach\\n\\n* Audit actual scope enforcement.\\n* Establish the canonical scope-presentation catalog.\\n* Add authenticated-user summary.\\n* Add explicit current workspace-reach explanation.\\n* Add accessibility and regression tests.\\n\\n### Phase 2 — Workspace-binding feasibility\\n\\n* Inspect OAuth grant/token and MCP authorization plumbing.\\n* Decide the binding model.\\n* Threat-model enumeration, membership changes, revocation, and bypass paths.\\n* Write a compatibility and migration decision record.\\n\\n### Phase 3 — Selector and enforcement, if approved\\n\\n* Add server-validated eligible workspace choices.\\n* Bind the selection to the authorization transaction and grant.\\n* Enforce it across all MCP tools.\\n* Add audit events, negative tests, and end-to-end authorization tests.\\n\\n### Phase 4 — Rollout and observation\\n\\n* Roll out behind a flag or client allowlist if needed.\\n* Monitor authorization failures, denial rates, wrong-account recovery, and support volume.\\n* Expand only after enforcement and compatibility evidence are green.\\n\\n## Test strategy\\n\\n* Unit tests for every registered scope's presentation metadata.\\n* Policy tests tying descriptions to known operation categories.\\n* Server-rendering tests for identity and reach modes.\\n* Tampering tests for user, scope, callback, resource, and workspace parameters.\\n* End-to-end PKCE flows for approve, deny, switch account, stale session, and optional workspace selection.\\n* MCP integration tests proving cross-workspace access is rejected under a bound grant.\\n* Accessibility checks for keyboard, screen-reader labels, focus order, contrast, and responsive layouts.\\n* Audit tests confirming useful metadata is recorded and secrets are absent.\\n\\n## Migration and rollback\\n\\n* Copy and identity improvements can be backward-compatible.\\n* If workspace binding is introduced, explicitly decide whether existing grants remain unbound, require reauthorization, or are migrated.\\n* Keep a rollback path that disables selection while retaining truthful reach copy.\\n* Never roll back server enforcement while leaving the UI claiming a workspace restriction.\\n\\n## Risks and trade-offs\\n\\n* Scope copy can drift from implementation without catalog ownership and tests.\\n* Displaying too much identity information creates privacy risk; too little fails to prevent mistakes.\\n* Workspace binding improves least privilege but increases token/grant complexity and support burden.\\n* Membership changes require a deliberate invalidation or request-time policy.\\n* A phased release reduces risk but temporarily leaves workspace selection unavailable.\\n\\n## Open architecture questions\\n\\n* Where are scopes registered and enforced today?\\n* Does AuthFn support authorization-details-style constraints or only flat scopes?\\n* Are tokens self-contained, introspected, or both?\\n* Can the MCP server enforce a workspace claim uniformly across all tools?\\n* What is the expected behavior for personal workspaces?\\n* Does switching accounts require sign-out, account chooser, or transaction restart?\\n* How are consent and workspace selection events represented in audit history?\",\"icon\":null,\"color\":null,\"url\":\"https://linear.app/21n/document/architecture-and-delivery-plan-ski-4-clarify-mcp-authorization-804a7179efd6\",\"slugId\":\"804a7179efd6\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-08-05T12:30:49.242Z\",\"updatedAt\":\"2026-08-05T12:30:50.199Z\",\"archivedAt\":null,\"creator\":{\"id\":\"3a6ed003-6380-47ef-9e7b-b2b01ebddc6a\",\"name\":\"Aravind\"},\"updatedBy\":{\"id\":\"3a6ed003-6380-47ef-9e7b-b2b01ebddc6a\",\"name\":\"Aravind\"},\"project\":null,\"initiative\":null,\"team\":null,\"issue\":{\"id\":\"SKI-4\",\"title\":\"Clarify MCP authorization permissions and workspace identity\"}}"}]}}
{"name":"ski4-ideation","result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"id\":\"e92029da-4c5f-4905-8b5c-a0c2234d38db\",\"title\":\"Ideation & Product Rationale — SKI-4: Clarify MCP authorization permissions and workspace identity\",\"content\":\"# Ideation & Product Rationale\\n\\nBacklog issue: [SKI-4 — Clarify MCP authorization permissions and workspace identity](<https://linear.app/21n/issue/SKI-4/clarify-mcp-authorization-permissions-and-workspace-identity>)\\n\\n## Context\\n\\nThe MCP authorization page is the user's decision surface for granting Codex or another local/remote agent access to Skillplane. It currently presents the client, requested scopes, MCP resource, callback, and a local-callback warning, but s…3225 tokens truncated…th composition | Turnstile, recipient/network policy, UX mapping | Duplicate OTP lifecycle or client protocol |\\n| Schema generator | AuthFn table and plugin fields | Unreviewed destructive migrations |\\n| Shared middleware | Generic persistent rate-limit counters | Product-specific key and threshold policy |\\n| Internal cursor codec | Signing, encoding, verification, expiry | Surface-specific query semantics |\\n| Observability package | Retrieval-event mapping and persistence helpers | Product authorization decisions |\\n| Local Cloudflare SendFn adapter | Provider transport mapping | Parallel generic failure taxonomy |\\n\\n## Data and request flow\\n\\n### OTP send\\n\\n1. UI calls AuthFn client with normalized input and transient Turnstile evidence.\\n2. Native AuthFn route validates the protocol payload.\\n3. `beforeChallengeSend` verifies Turnstile and evaluates both rate-limit keys.\\n4. Policy-only transient data is removed before challenge metadata persists.\\n5. AuthFn invokes SendFn and returns its native response envelope.\\n6. Thin UI mapping presents success or a safe error.\\n\\n### OTP verify\\n\\n1. UI calls the native AuthFn verify method with cookie session mode.\\n2. AuthFn validates the challenge and issues the session.\\n3. Skillplane consumes the canonical result; it does not repeat protocol validation.\\n4. If a required pre-verify policy is unsupported, stop and design an upstream hook rather than shadowing the route.\\n\\n### Cursor verification\\n\\n1. Surface adapter supplies expected purpose, tenant, and filters.\\n2. Shared codec verifies signature and expiry.\\n3. Codec compares bound context and returns the typed position.\\n4. Surface maps failures to its protocol-specific error.\\n\\n## Security and privacy\\n\\n* Never persist or log Turnstile tokens.\\n* Retain fail-closed behavior for verification and rate-limit infrastructure failures.\\n* Use constant-time signature comparison.\\n* Bind cursors to purpose and tenant; bind filters where the surface is filter-sensitive.\\n* Rotate or version cursor secrets and payloads deliberately.\\n* Keep user-visible auth errors safe while preserving internal correlation.\\n* Review generated schema and migrations for accidental nullability, index, or constraint changes.\\n\\n## Reliability and observability\\n\\n* Emit distinct events for policy rejection, provider failure, invalid challenge, session failure, and internal failure.\\n* Preserve request correlation through native package extension points.\\n* Add metrics for OTP sends, policy rejects, provider failures, successful verifications, and cursor decode failures.\\n* Avoid logging recipient values where hashed or redacted dimensions suffice.\\n* Add batch audit persistence only behind a single transaction owner.\\n\\n## Migration and rollback\\n\\n* Land changes in small, reversible phases.\\n* Do not remove custom routes/client/schema until parity tests pass against native paths.\\n* If cursor compatibility is required, temporarily decode legacy versions and issue only the new version.\\n* If schema generation differs, inspect and stage migrations explicitly before changing runtime schema imports.\\n* Keep the old rate-limit table until shared persistence is proven and migration/cleanup is approved.\\n* Roll back by restoring the previous composition layer; do not roll back database state destructively.\\n\\n## Delivery phases\\n\\n1. **Version and contract verification**\\n   * Reverify released AuthFn, SendFn, and middleware versions and APIs.\\n   * Record the ownership matrix and exact parity requirements.\\n2. **AuthFn route/client migration**\\n   * Add released client dependency.\\n   * Move pre-send policy into supported hooks.\\n   * Switch UI calls to native routes.\\n   * Preserve cookie sessions, correlation, and required headers.\\n   * Remove shadows only after parity passes.\\n3. **Schema generation**\\n   * Generate from the composed plugin set.\\n   * Compare with current Drizzle definitions.\\n   * Review migration effects and add a CI drift gate.\\n4. **Rate-limit persistence**\\n   * Adapt the two-key policy to shared middleware.\\n   * Run concurrency, expiry, and failure-mode tests.\\n   * Remove generic local storage only after parity.\\n5. **Internal security consolidation**\\n   * Introduce the purpose-aware cursor codec.\\n   * Migrate MCP, search, and audit consumers.\\n   * Consolidate retrieval audit mapping and batch behavior.\\n6. **SendFn normalization**\\n   * Replace custom failure classes with native semantics.\\n   * Preserve the local provider boundary.\\n   * Add provider failure tests.\\n7. **Integrated validation and rollout**\\n   * Run typechecks, unit/integration tests, browser-critical auth flows, database migration checks, and MCP regression.\\n   * Decide legacy cursor compatibility.\\n   * Roll out with auth and error telemetry monitored.\\n\\n## Validation plan\\n\\n* Unit tests for hook policy, cursor codec, error adapters, and generated-schema drift.\\n* Hono integration tests proving native AuthFn endpoints and cookie-session behavior.\\n* Browser E2E for OTP send, invalid code, expired code, success, rate limit, Turnstile failure, and provider failure.\\n* Database tests for concurrent rate limits and schema migration safety.\\n* MCP and audit pagination tests across purpose, tenant, filter, expiry, and tamper cases.\\n* SendFn adapter tests for native success and failure shapes.\\n* Static reachability checks that fail if the app restores a parallel AuthFn client or OTP route shadow.\\n\\n## Dependencies\\n\\n* [SKI-1 — Move service-principal credential authentication to AuthFn API keys](<https://linear.app/21n/issue/SKI-1/move-service-principal-credential-authentication-to-authfn-api-keys>)\\n* [SKI-2 — Adopt DataFn in Skillplane's first-party application data path](<https://linear.app/21n/issue/SKI-2/adopt-datafn-in-skillplanes-first-party-application-data-path>)\\n* [SKI-6 — Adopt McpFn for MCP regression and conformance coverage](<https://linear.app/21n/issue/SKI-6/adopt-mcpfn-for-mcp-regression-and-conformance-coverage>)\\n* Released AuthFn client/Svelte packages, schema generator, Superfunctions middleware, and SendFn package at implementation-time verified versions.\\n\\n## Risks and tradeoffs\\n\\n* Native AuthFn behavior may differ subtly from current response envelopes.\\n* Missing hooks could tempt route shadowing; prefer an upstream extension proposal.\\n* Cursor consolidation can invalidate in-flight pages.\\n* Generated schema may reveal drift requiring careful migration.\\n* Shared rate-limit persistence may not map cleanly to the current database adapter.\\n* A broad refactor could obscure regressions; phase work by boundary and retain explicit parity gates.\\n\\n## Alternatives\\n\\n* Keep all local implementations: lowest immediate change, highest ongoing drift.\\n* Upstream every Skillplane policy into Superfunctions: rejected because product-specific concerns would pollute generic packages.\\n* Perform one large replacement branch: rejected because rollback and attribution would be poor.\\n* Defer all work to McpFn/DataFn migrations: rejected because AuthFn, schema, cursor, audit, and SendFn duplication are independently actionable now.\\n\\n## Assumptions and open questions\\n\\n* **Assumption:** Exact package APIs remain compatible with the audited versions; verify before implementation.\\n* **Open question:** Header extension point for native OTP responses.\\n* **Open question:** Legacy cursor compatibility window.\\n* **Open question:** Shared middleware adapter fit and any upstream change.\\n* **Open question:** Whether browser auth state benefits from `@authfn/svelte` beyond `@authfn/client`.\\n* **Open question:** Timing and ownership of upstreaming the Cloudflare provider.\\n\\n## Completion evidence\\n\\nCompletion requires source and test evidence for every acceptance criterion. A green focused suite does not by itself prove deployed behavior; local, browser, database, tunnel, and deployed verification must be reported separately.\",\"icon\":null,\"color\":null,\"url\":\"https://linear.app/21n/document/architecture-and-delivery-plan-ski-10-remove-duplicated-framework-and-58a68c622bf3\",\"slugId\":\"58a68c622bf3\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-08-22T05:52:37.647Z\",\"updatedAt\":\"2026-08-22T05:52:38.800Z\",\"archivedAt\":null,\"creator\":{\"id\":\"3a6ed003-6380-47ef-9e7b-b2b01ebddc6a\",\"name\":\"Aravind\"},\"updatedBy\":{\"id\":\"3a6ed003-6380-47ef-9e7b-b2b01ebddc6a\",\"name\":\"Aravind\"},\"project\":null,\"initiative\":null,\"team\":null,\"issue\":{\"id\":\"SKI-10\",\"title\":\"Remove duplicated framework and security primitives from Skillplane\"}}"}]}}
{"name":"ski10-ideation","result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"id\":\"bebfbf64-1208-4b7b-a3f6-cd5238526276\",\"title\":\"Ideation & Product Rationale — SKI-10: Remove duplicated framework and security primitives from Skillplane\",\"content\":\"# Ideation & Product Rationale\\n\\nIssue: [SKI-10 — Remove duplicated framework and security primitives from Skillplane](<https://linear.app/21n/issue/SKI-10/remove-duplicated-framework-and-security-primitives-from-skillplane>)\\n\\n## Context and motivating problem\\n\\nA full-repository audit on 2026-08-22 found that Skillplane genuinely composes several Superfunctions packages but still owns parallel framework contracts around authentication, schema, rate limiting, cursors, audit mapping, and email-provider failures.\\n\\nThe issue is not that Skillplane has product-specific domain code. Workspace authorization, skill publication, immutable bundles, OAuth authorization-server behavior, audit retention, and authorization-aware search remain legitimate Skillplane responsibilities. The problem is duplicated generic behavior that can drift from the packages already selected as canonical.\\n\\nThe largest user-facing risk is authentication drift: Skillplane shadows AuthFn OTP routes and implements a parallel browser client. The other findings primarily increase security review cost, migration risk, and maintenance burden.\\n\\n## Target users and stakeholders\\n\\n* Skillplane end users who need reliable OTP login and stable sessions.\\n* MCP clients and service principals that depend on consistent authorization and error behavior.\\n* Skillplane engineers maintaining product logic and migrations.\\n* Superfunctions maintainers responsible for reusable AuthFn, SendFn, middleware, and schema-generation contracts.\\n* Operators reviewing security, rate limiting, auditability, and incident behavior.\\n\\n## Use cases and journeys\\n\\n### OTP sign-in\\n\\n1. A user enters an email in Skillplane.\\n2. Skillplane invokes the released AuthFn client.\\n3. AuthFn owns request validation, challenge issuance, verification, and session behavior.\\n4. Skillplane policy runs through supported hooks: Turnstile validation and recipient/network rate limits.\\n5. A successful verification establishes the expected cookie session without a parallel response contract.\\n\\n### Schema evolution\\n\\n1. The composed AuthFn plugin set is the source of schema truth.\\n2. The Superfunctions generator emits deterministic Drizzle schema.\\n3. Reviewers inspect the generated migration.\\n4. CI fails when committed schema and generated output drift.\\n\\n### Pagination and audit retrieval\\n\\n1. A product surface requests a page with filters.\\n2. One internal cursor codec signs purpose, tenant, filters, and expiry.\\n3. Protocol-specific adapters map codec failures to Hono, MCP, or domain errors.\\n4. Retrieval events use one central mapping before persistence.\\n\\n### Email delivery failure\\n\\n1. AuthFn requests OTP delivery through the local Cloudflare SendFn adapter.\\n2. The adapter returns or throws SendFn-native failure semantics.\\n3. AuthFn and Skillplane map the canonical error without parallel custom classes.\\n\\n## Ideas discussed\\n\\n* Use native AuthFn OTP routes rather than Hono route shadows.\\n* Replace the custom browser auth client with `@authfn/client`; use `@authfn/svelte` only where its state bindings add value.\\n* Move Turnstile and two-key rate-limit policy into `beforeChallengeSend`; retain only minimal additive middleware if response headers cannot be expressed by the hook.\\n* Generate AuthFn schema from the composed plugin set.\\n* Compose product-specific recipient/network policy over shared rate-limit persistence.\\n* Consolidate three cursor implementations into one purpose-aware codec.\\n* Reuse the exported retrieval audit mapper.\\n* Keep the local Cloudflare provider for now, but normalize it to SendFn contracts.\\n* Track DataFn and McpFn separately in <issue id=\\\"4056b3f8-de07-41bb-aad7-e3537d397b93\\\" href=\\\"https://linear.app/21n/issue/SKI-2/adopt-datafn-in-skillplanes-first-party-application-data-path\\\">SKI-2</issue> and <issue id=\\\"aaf7021f-756f-4919-ad1c-830c2fa250f8\\\" href=\\\"https://linear.app/21n/issue/SKI-6/adopt-mcpfn-for-mcp-regression-and-conformance-coverage\\\">SKI-6</issue>.\\n\\n## Desired outcomes and success measures\\n\\n* No local owner for AuthFn OTP send or verify routes.\\n* No parallel browser implementation of the AuthFn protocol.\\n* Authentication parity tests prove cookie sessions, validation, fail-closed policy, and safe Turnstile handling.\\n* Generated AuthFn schema has a reproducible drift gate.\\n* Only one cursor-signing implementation remains.\\n* Retrieval audit mapping has one canonical owner.\\n* SendFn failures have one error contract.\\n* The reduction in duplicate code does not move Skillplane-specific invariants into generic packages.\\n\\n## Decisions and rationale\\n\\n* **Decision:** Keep this issue separate from DataFn adoption.\\n  **Rationale:** <issue id=\\\"4056b3f8-de07-41bb-aad7-e3537d397b93\\\" href=\\\"https://linear.app/21n/issue/SKI-2/adopt-datafn-in-skillplanes-first-party-application-data-path\\\">SKI-2</issue> already captures DataFn reachability and read/command ownership.\\n* **Decision:** Keep McpFn migration separate.\\n  **Rationale:** <issue id=\\\"aaf7021f-756f-4919-ad1c-830c2fa250f8\\\" href=\\\"https://linear.app/21n/issue/SKI-6/adopt-mcpfn-for-mcp-regression-and-conformance-coverage\\\">SKI-6</issue> already captures it, and the audited package was not available at the checked npm identifier.\\n* **Decision:** Preserve the local Cloudflare provider initially.\\n  **Rationale:** Local placement was an accepted boundary around overlapping external work; error-contract normalization can happen without an upstream write.\\n* **Decision:** Preserve Skillplane's OAuth authorization-server plugin.\\n  **Rationale:** AuthFn social OAuth and resource-server middleware do not replace consent, authorization-code issuance, refresh-family rotation, and workspace policy.\\n* **Decision:** Prefer hooks/plugins/clients over route overrides.\\n  **Rationale:** This keeps validation, session, and protocol behavior on the package's supported upgrade path.\\n\\n## Rejected alternatives and tradeoffs\\n\\n* **Keep route shadows because they currently work:** rejected as the default because it preserves duplicated validation, envelopes, request IDs, and error mapping.\\n* **Move all auth policy into AuthFn core:** rejected because Turnstile and product-specific recipient/network policy belong in consumer composition unless a broadly reusable hook is missing.\\n* **Replace all Hono/domain APIs with DataFn:** rejected; invariant-heavy commands remain domain workflows.\\n* **Replace all Skillplane storage/search code with FileFn/SearchFn immediately:** rejected pending fit tests for immutable canonical bundles and authorization-filtered search.\\n* **Bundle McpFn migration into this issue:** rejected to avoid mixing a larger transport/registry modernization with current released-package corrections.\\n\\n## Product, UX, and operational considerations\\n\\n* OTP response and error behavior must remain understandable and stable for the UI.\\n* Rate-limit headers may be useful for diagnostics but must not require ownership of the entire OTP route.\\n* Cursor invalidation can affect pagination continuity after deployment; the rollout decision must be explicit.\\n* Schema regeneration must be reviewed like a migration, not treated as a formatting change.\\n* Observability must distinguish policy rejection, provider failure, invalid OTP, and internal failure without logging secrets or raw Turnstile tokens.\\n\\n## Goals\\n\\n* Reduce duplicate framework and security logic.\\n* Make package ownership explicit and upgradeable.\\n* Preserve product-specific security policy and domain invariants.\\n* Add evidence that production surfaces traverse the intended integration.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n\\n* <issue id=\\\"4056b3f8-de07-41bb-aad7-e3537d397b93\\\" href=\\\"https://linear.app/21n/issue/SKI-2/adopt-datafn-in-skillplanes-first-party-application-data-path\\\">SKI-2</issue> DataFn application adoption.\\n* <issue id=\\\"693fecfe-0647-47d3-a152-20874fb3fad5\\\" href=\\\"https://linear.app/21n/issue/SKI-1/move-service-principal-credential-authentication-to-authfn-api-keys\\\">SKI-1</issue> service-principal API-key work.\\n* <issue id=\\\"aaf7021f-756f-4919-ad1c-830c2fa250f8\\\" href=\\\"https://linear.app/21n/issue/SKI-6/adopt-mcpfn-for-mcp-regression-and-conformance-coverage\\\">SKI-6</issue> McpFn adoption.\\n* Upstream package changes without separate authorization.\\n* Product redesign, staffing, dates, estimates, or deployment commitments.\\n\\n## Assumptions\\n\\n* **Assumption:** The released package versions still expose the APIs observed in the audit; implementation must reverify exact versions.\\n* **Assumption:** Native AuthFn routes support the required cookie-session mode.\\n* **Assumption:** A minimal response middleware can preserve required headers if AuthFn hooks cannot emit them.\\n\\n## Open questions\\n\\n* Is an upstream AuthFn response hook preferable to thin additive middleware?\\n* Must already-issued cursors remain valid across migration?\\n* Does shared rate-limit persistence support the current Postgres adapter directly?\\n* Which parity tests should be browser-level versus Hono integration tests?\\n* Should the Cloudflare provider be upstreamed after local normalization?\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n* The acceptance criteria in <issue id=\\\"829274d3-c021-40ad-8082-91911d541592\\\" href=\\\"https://linear.app/21n/issue/SKI-10/remove-duplicated-framework-and-security-primitives-from-skillplane\\\">SKI-10</issue> are met and read back from tests and source.\\n* No Turnstile token is stored in challenge metadata or logs.\\n* Package-owned and Skillplane-owned responsibilities are documented.\\n* <issue id=\\\"693fecfe-0647-47d3-a152-20874fb3fad5\\\" href=\\\"https://linear.app/21n/issue/SKI-1/move-service-principal-credential-authentication-to-authfn-api-keys\\\">SKI-1</issue>, <issue id=\\\"4056b3f8-de07-41bb-aad7-e3537d397b93\\\" href=\\\"https://linear.app/21n/issue/SKI-2/adopt-datafn-in-skillplanes-first-party-application-data-path\\\">SKI-2</issue>, and <issue id=\\\"aaf7021f-756f-4919-ad1c-830c2fa250f8\\\" href=\\\"https://linear.app/21n/issue/SKI-6/adopt-mcpfn-for-mcp-regression-and-conformance-coverage\\\">SKI-6</issue> remain separate related work rather than being silently absorbed.\",\"icon\":null,\"color\":null,\"url\":\"https://linear.app/21n/document/ideation-and-product-rationale-ski-10-remove-duplicated-framework-and-6b49cd027863\",\"slugId\":\"6b49cd027863\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-08-22T05:52:34.996Z\",\"updatedAt\":\"2026-08-22T05:52:35.899Z\",\"archivedAt\":null,\"creator\":{\"id\":\"3a6ed003-6380-47ef-9e7b-b2b01ebddc6a\",\"name\":\"Aravind\"},\"updatedBy\":{\"id\":\"3a6ed003-6380-47ef-9e7b-b2b01ebddc6a\",\"name\":\"Aravind\"},\"project\":null,\"initiative\":null,\"team\":null,\"issue\":{\"id\":\"SKI-10\",\"title\":\"Remove duplicated framework and security primitives from Skillplane\"}}"}]}}

